+## Bitrate Analysis Explained
+
+### What is Bitrate?
+
+**Simple explanation:** Bitrate is how much data is used per second of video.
+
+```
+Bitrate = File Size ÷ Duration
+
+Example:
+- File size: 72 MB (megabytes)
+- Duration: 97 seconds
+- Bitrate: 72 MB ÷ 97 sec = 0.74 MB/sec = 5.9 Mbps
+```
+
+### Why Bitrate Matters
+
+| Higher Bitrate | Lower Bitrate |
+|----------------|---------------|
+| Better quality | Worse quality |
+| Larger files | Smaller files |
+| More bandwidth | Less bandwidth |
+| Expensive streaming | Cheaper streaming |
+
+### Our Bitrate Metrics
+
+#### 1. Reference Bitrate
+```python
+ref_bitrate = ref_info.get("bitrate", 0)
+ref_mbps = ref_bitrate / 1_000_000  # Convert to Megabits per second
+```
+- The original video's bitrate
+- Higher = better source quality
+
+#### 2. Distorted Bitrate
+```python
+dist_bitrate = dist_info.get("bitrate", 0)
+dist_mbps = dist_bitrate / 1_000_000
+```
+- The compressed video's bitrate
+- This is what viewers actually download
+
+#### 3. Bitrate Reduction
+```python
+reduction = ((ref_bitrate - dist_bitrate) / ref_bitrate) * 100
+# Example: ((6.25 - 2.31) / 6.25) * 100 = 63.1%
+```
+- How much smaller the compressed video is
+- 63% reduction = you only need 37% of original bandwidth
+
+#### 4. Encoding Efficiency
+```python
+efficiency = vmaf_score / dist_mbps
+# Example: 95.41 / 2.31 = 41.4 VMAF per Mbps
+```
+- Quality per bandwidth
+- Higher = better encoder/settings
+- Useful for comparing different encoding settings
+
+#### 5. Bandwidth Saved
+```python
+savings = ref_mbps - dist_mbps
+# Example: 6.25 - 2.31 = 3.94 Mbps saved
+```
+- Actual bandwidth reduction
+- Multiply by viewers = total bandwidth saved
+
+### Practical Example
+
+```
+Your video streaming scenario:
+- Original: 6.25 Mbps
+- Compressed: 2.31 Mbps
+- Viewers: 10,000 concurrent
+
+Bandwidth needed:
+- Without compression: 6.25 × 10,000 = 62,500 Mbps = 62.5 Gbps
+- With compression: 2.31 × 10,000 = 23,100 Mbps = 23.1 Gbps
+- Savings: 39.4 Gbps (63% less!)
+```
